{
  "title": "Trig Values of Standard Angles — The Power Table ⚡",
  "hook": "There are exactly 5 angles whose trig values you MUST memorize: 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90°. They appear in almost every board exam question! But here's a trick — you only need to remember ONE row, and you can derive the rest!",
  "explanation": "<div style=\"background:linear-gradient(135deg,#E8EAF6,#C5CAE9);border-radius:12px;padding:16px;margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"font-weight:700;text-align:center;margin-bottom:10px;color:#283593\">📊 THE POWER TABLE</div><table style=\"width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0;font-size:0.88rem\"><tr><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">θ →</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">0°</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">30°</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">45°</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">60°</th><th style=\"background:#E3F2FD;padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #BBDEFB;text-align:left;font-weight:700\">90°</th></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\"><strong>sin θ</strong></td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">0</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1/2</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1/√2</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">√3/2</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\"><strong>cos θ</strong></td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">√3/2</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1/√2</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1/2</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">0</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\"><strong>tan θ</strong></td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">0</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1/√3</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">1</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">√3</td><td style=\"padding:8px 12px;border:1px solid #E0E0E0\">∞ (undefined)</td></tr></table></div>\n\n<div style=\"background:#FFF8E1;border-radius:10px;padding:14px;margin:12px 0\"><strong>🧠 The Memory Trick (for sin θ):</strong><br><br>Write: √0/2, √1/2, √2/2, √3/2, √4/2 for θ = 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90°<br><br><div style=\"display:flex;gap:6px;justify-content:center;flex-wrap:wrap\"><div style=\"background:white;border:2px solid #F9A825;border-radius:8px;padding:8px 12px;text-align:center;min-width:55px\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;color:#666\">0°</div><div style=\"font-weight:bold\">√0/2</div><div style=\"font-size:0.8rem\">=0</div></div><div style=\"background:white;border:2px solid #F9A825;border-radius:8px;padding:8px 12px;text-align:center;min-width:55px\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;color:#666\">30°</div><div style=\"font-weight:bold\">√1/2</div><div style=\"font-size:0.8rem\">=1/2</div></div><div style=\"background:white;border:2px solid #F9A825;border-radius:8px;padding:8px 12px;text-align:center;min-width:55px\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;color:#666\">45°</div><div style=\"font-weight:bold\">√2/2</div><div style=\"font-size:0.8rem\">=1/√2</div></div><div style=\"background:white;border:2px solid #F9A825;border-radius:8px;padding:8px 12px;text-align:center;min-width:55px\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;color:#666\">60°</div><div style=\"font-weight:bold\">√3/2</div><div style=\"font-size:0.8rem\">=√3/2</div></div><div style=\"background:white;border:2px solid #F9A825;border-radius:8px;padding:8px 12px;text-align:center;min-width:55px\"><div style=\"font-size:0.7rem;color:#666\">90°</div><div style=\"font-weight:bold\">√4/2</div><div style=\"font-size:0.8rem\">=1</div></div></div><br>For <strong>cos θ</strong>: just <strong>reverse</strong> the row! cos 0° = sin 90° = 1, etc.<br>For <strong>tan θ</strong>: just divide sin/cos for each angle.</div>\n\n<div style=\"background:#FFF8E1;border-left:4px solid #FFB300;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;margin:10px 0;font-size:0.88rem\">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> Memorize the sin row using √0, √1, √2, √3, √4 (all divided by 2). Then cos is the reverse, and tan = sin/cos!</div>",
  "worked_example": "<strong>Evaluate: sin 60° cos 30° + sin 30° cos 60°</strong>\n\n<div style=\"margin:12px 0\"><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#1565C0;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">1</div>\n<div><strong>Substitute values</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">= (√3/2)(√3/2) + (1/2)(1/2)</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#1565C0;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">2</div>\n<div><strong>Multiply</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">= 3/4 + 1/4</span></div></div><div style=\"display:flex;gap:12px;margin-bottom:10px;align-items:flex-start\">\n<div style=\"min-width:32px;height:32px;border-radius:50%;background:#1565C0;color:white;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;font-weight:700;font-size:0.85rem\">3</div>\n<div><strong>Add</strong><br><span style=\"color:#555;font-size:0.88rem\">= <strong>4/4 = 1</strong></span></div></div></div><div style=\"background:#FFF8E1;border-left:4px solid #FFB300;border-radius:8px;padding:10px 14px;margin:10px 0;font-size:0.88rem\">💡 <strong>Tip:</strong> Notice this equals sin(60° + 30°) = sin 90° = 1. This is actually the sine addition formula in action!</div>",
  "try_this": {
    "question": "Evaluate: (tan 30°)(tan 60°) + cos²45° + sin²45°",
    "hint": "tan 30° = 1/√3, tan 60° = √3, cos 45° = sin 45° = 1/√2",
    "answer": "(1/√3)(√3) + (1/√2)² + (1/√2)²\n= 1 + 1/2 + 1/2\n= 1 + 1 = 2"
  },
  "fun_fact": "The ancient Indian mathematician Aryabhata (476 CE) created the first known table of sine values! He called sine 'ardha-jya' (half-chord) and his values for 24 angles from 0° to 90° were accurate to about 4 decimal places — without a calculator! 🧮"
}